+It has labels, conditional jumps, and support for C identifiers.  In fact,
+C identifiers are just passed on to the C compiler.
+
+It also has an "ifeqdef" statement, which wraps each "ifeq" in an ifdef.
+This lets us use the same filters for all platforms - if it doesn't support
+a syscall, its "allow" rule just doesn't get compiled in.
+
+Ideally, the filter should be constructed by sorting the syscalls in order
+of usage frequency and then checking each syscall with ifeqdef.
+
+The assembler runs in four steps:
+
+* audit_nr.h is generated by compiling a dummy program and parsing its ELF
+  header.  This is included from common.chasc, which checks the audit arch
+  number at the top of the filter.
+* gen_defs generates a chasc file with C preprocessor directives from
+  `$<.chasc`.
+* The C preprocessor reads the chasc file, disabling filters for syscalls
+  that do not apply to this platform.  The output is in `$<.chasc.expanded`.
+* gen_syscalls takes `$<.chasc.expanded` and outputs `chasc_$<.h`.  This is
+  the final header file we include in the actual program.
